type": "disagreement", "problem": "Why the 'helpful' prompt reduces false answers, and whether this reflects real understanding or simulation of a professor.", "did": "Suggested part of the benefit comes from the model saying 'I have no comment' more often (counted as true), which needs no sophisticated simulation, while noting non-rigorous evidence of some prompt understanding.", "alternatives_named": ["the model coherently simulating a diligent professor"], "stated_reason": "Non-committal answers are counted as true, so the effect does not require sophisticated understanding."}, {"key": "d6", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to judge whether prompt engineering will fix imitative falsehoods in current models.", "did": "Cited the small variation across prompts on percent true+informative and prior work showing tuned few-shot prompts barely beat random ones, concluding he is less optimistic than the reviewer about prompts, while expecting more benefit for larger or instruction-finetuned models.", "alternatives_named": ["better prompts as an easy fix"], "stated_reason": "Results across prompts varied little on true+informative, and tuned prompts don't generally perform much better than random ones."}, {"key": "d7", "type": "advice", "problem": "Whether a single illustrative failure example (the Asimov question) is fixed by the best prompt.", "did": "Checked the Asimov question across all prompts rather than only the helpful one, finding the helpful prompt did no better than others.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "Comparing across prompts shows whether the helpful prompt actually fixes the example."}, {"key": "d8", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to reduce the cost of human evaluation in the TruthfulQA benchmark.", "did": "Provided reference true and false answers with citations and multiple-choice versions of the task to make evaluation quicker.", "alternatives_named": ["purely human evaluation", "automatic evaluation using GPT-3"], "stated_reason": "Reference answers make human evaluation easy when outputs resemble them, and multiple-choice versions are quick and straightforward."}, {"key": "d9", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to address concerns that truth-evaluation mechanisms are politically captured or biased.", "did": "Treats capture as a matter of degree and recommends measuring the percentage of false or biased answers per mechanism to inform evaluation design for AI.", "alternatives_named": ["treating capture as all-or-nothing"], "stated_reason": "Some mechanisms would score much better than others, and most questions (natural sciences, math, local questions) don't depend on controversial politics."}, {"key": "d10", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to weigh a claim that a small company's DeepL translator beats Google Translate.", "did": "Asked for a quantitative study by external researchers and discounted the developer's own comparisons for lacking methodology and showing implausibly large gaps.", "alternatives_named": ["the developer's own quality comparison page"], "stated_reason": "The company's comparison gave no information about methodology and the differences seemed too big to be credible."}, {"key": "d11", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to assess whether being based in the Bay Area affects the quality of alignment research, compared with other locations.", "did": "He recommends an empirical comparison of matched organizations (Bay vs London/Oxford/Cambridge) using the overlap in personnel, rather than relying on general arguments.", "alternatives_named": ["general arguments that the Bay Area is expensive and has bad epistemics"], "stated_reason": "Although people are not randomly assigned to organizations, the personnel overlap is enough for the observational evidence to likely be meaningful."}, {"key": "d12", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to estimate the value of the full proprietary CYC knowledge base without buying it.", "did": "He suggests finetuning a model on the publicly released subsets of CYC and using the result to estimate the value of the full dataset.", "alternatives_named": ["buying out Cyc", "using other open-source knowledge bases", "collaborating with researchers at companies holding closed knowledge graphs"], "stated_reason": ""}, {"key": "d13", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to interpret GPT-4chan's TruthfulQA score, which was reported as outperforming GPT-3.", "did": "He compares the score to the random-guessing baseline and to a stronger model, showing GPT-4chan is worse than chance, and recommends the generation task over multiple-choice.", "alternatives_named": ["evaluating with the multiple-choice (MC1) task"], "stated_reason": "A score equal to or below chance is not evidence of truthfulness, and the generation task is what DeepMind and OpenAI used to evaluate their models."}, {"key": "d14", "type": "disagreement", "problem": "Whether surpassing top human scores on BIG-bench should be taken as showing a model is an AGI.", "did": "He challenges the claim that BIG-bench is a final AGI benchmark, noting PaLM already beats average humans on many tasks, the multiple-choice format favors LMs, and training-data leakage is likely.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "PaLM already beats the human average on 150 tasks with the curve not bending, most tasks are multiple-choice (favorable to LMs), and some tasks will likely leak into training data."}, {"key": "d15", "type": "decision", "problem": "Whether to extend the uncertainty-expression paper to judgmental forecasting such as Metaculus.", "did": "They did not test forecasting, keeping the paper focused on expressing the model's own uncertainty in natural language rather than improving forecasting ability.", "alternatives_named": ["evaluating on Metaculus forecasting", "getting better at judgmental forecasting"], "stated_reason": "The paper's focus is on expressing the model's own uncertainty, and finetuning on simple math questions likely wouldn't help forecasting."}, {"key": "d16", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to train the model to give verbalized probabilities, given that cross-entropy against the ground-truth answer is only usable for the indirect logit.", "did": "They used supervised learning with the empirical accuracy for each question type as the labels for verbalized probability, avoiding RL.", "alternatives_named": ["training verbalized probability with RL", "cross-entropy on ground-truth correct answers"], "stated_reason": "Cross-entropy on the ground-truth answer can't be used for verbalized probability without using RL."}, {"key": "d17", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to probe whether GPT-3's in-context model-fitting ability reflects memorization of training data.", "did": "He suggests testing whether GPT-3 can regenerate parts of well-known datasets like Iris, and adding finetuning, InstructGPT and embedding analyses.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": ""}, {"key": "d18", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to evaluate a model's answer on a task when a single sampled output may hide how confident or consistent the model is.", "did": "Recommends looking at the model's output probabilities or taking multiple samples rather than relying on one answer.", "alternatives_named": ["looking at a single sampled answer"], "stated_reason": "Probabilities distinguish a 99% confident answer from one at close to 50%."}, {"key": "d19", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to set forecast uncertainty for a new benchmark (MATH) when only weak early results exist.", "did": "Recommends a wider distribution over future results and using base rates from how benchmarks improve after their introduction (e.g. plotting many benchmarks).", "alternatives_named": ["narrow forecasts anchored on the initial weak GPT-2/GPT-3 results"], "stated_reason": "Performance was very low and few models/setups had been tried, and benchmarks like Lambada jumped quickly (7.3% to 49% in a year)."}, {"key": "d20", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to test claims about language models' learning ability relative to humans.", "did": "Suggests measuring GPT-3's level across many foreign languages and comparing to known human time-to-proficiency as a benchmark.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "We know roughly how long humans take to reach a given level in a foreign language, and foreign languages are a small fraction of training data."}, {"key": "d21", "type": "disagreement", "problem": "How much to update on a single narrow paper (in-context RL) when critics suggest the result can be explained away.", "did": "Reframes the evaluation around the main question of interest and asks how the results update beliefs on it, acknowledging both explanations remain possible.", "alternatives_named": ["explaining the result away entirely"], "stated_reason": "The paper covers narrow tasks, but future work will extend and iterate on the setup."}, {"key": "d22", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to fairly compare Google Search and ChatGPT as search tools.", "did": "Recommends comparing them on the actual distribution of queries sent to Google rather than on programmer-style examples.", "alternatives_named": ["comparing on power-user or programming queries"], "stated_reason": "Many Google queries are navigational, translation or local-information queries, and power users have different usage distributions than average users."}, {"key": "d23", "type": "rejected", "problem": "Whether to train language models on curated, high-quality scientific text to make them smarter.", "did": "Points to Galactica's limited benefits as evidence that curated-data training is less useful than training on a bigger dataset plus techniques to access the scientific parts.", "alternatives_named": ["training on a curated dataset of scientific papers", "training on a bigger dataset and using other techniques to access the science parts"], "stated_reason": "The benefits of the curated-data approach in Galactica seem limited."}, {"key": "d24", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to evaluate whether activation steering truly reduces sycophancy and improves truthfulness on TruthfulQA.", "did": "Asks for overall accuracy (informative*truthful) for the unsteered base model with different prompts, and for positive and negative steering.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": ""}, {"key": "d25", "type": "advice", "problem": "Whether a steering-vector effect is specific to sycophancy or would arise from any steering direction.", "did": "Suggests an ablation using random steering vectors (e.g. love/hate, big/small) as a control.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "TruthfulQA often has two salient answers, so random steering could nudge the model from one to the other."}, {"key": "d26", "type": "advice", "problem": "Whether steering is better than simple prompting as a baseline for reducing sycophancy.", "did": "Recommends comparing steering to a prompt that gives a verbal instruction not to be sycophantic, and trying it on instruction-tuned and stronger models.", "alternatives_named": ["the TruthfulQA helpful prompt focused on truthfulness"], "stated_reason": "The standard helpful prompt targets truthfulness rather than avoiding sycophancy per se, and an instruction-based prompt might work better for instruction-tuned or stronger models."}, {"key": "d27", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to treat task-to-task variation in out-of-context learning scaling results across only 7 tasks.", "did": "Accepted the variation as typical of scaling results and ran a replication on a disjoint set of tasks to check the claims generalize.", "alternatives_named": ["treating the variation as a point against clean explanations", "using hundreds of tasks like BIG-Bench"], "stated_reason": "Individual tasks are noisy but averages are smooth (as in BIG-Bench), and a replication on a disjoint task set showed similar scaling."}, {"key": "d28", "type": "disagreement", "problem": "Whether small-model success on the hhh task reflects mere word association rather than real conceptual connections.", "did": "Used a replication on tasks excluding hhh, where Babbage beat Ada, to argue small models do more than simplest associative generalization, while conceding they are worse than davinci at explaining themselves.", "alternatives_named": ["small models only doing word association and parroting"], "stated_reason": "The replication without hhh still showed small models doing better with scale."}, {"key": "d29", "type": "disagreement", "problem": "Whether LLMs trained on passive text can robustly learn causal reasoning, given claims that they lack experimentation.", "did": "Argues scaling plus pre-training data (and external tools) can plausibly yield robust causal reasoning, citing GPT-3's few-shot generalization on math word problems.", "alternatives_named": ["the view that scaling cannot produce the robustness of human causal reasoning"], "stated_reason": "GPT-3 generalizes few-shot to math problems it wasn't finetuned on, an amazing level of robustness relative to 2018 systems."}, {"key": "d30", "type": "decision", "problem": "The paper aims at situational awareness, but its experiments might be read as measuring a model's sense of self.", "did": "He framed the experiments as tests of out-of-context reasoning, not situational awareness, and used multiple fictitious chatbot personas to avoid a self-identity reading.", "alternatives_named": ["framing the experiments as measuring situational awareness or a sense of self"], "stated_reason": "The motivation was to understand and forecast situational awareness, but the experiments only test a precursor, out-of-context reasoning."}]}
